window.docContents[8].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Kernel.Time/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Kernel___Time":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Kernel › Time.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 29 of 58 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nClock and Time SyscallsTrusted model of the Linux AArch64 clock_gettime syscall (kernel/time/posix-timers.c, include/uapi/linux/time.h), defining SYS_CLOCK_GETTIME, the 16-byte struct timespec encoder encodeTimespec, and execClockGettime.\n\n\n\ndef SYS_CLOCK_GETTIMEL26–27 ↗#Linux AArch64 clock_gettime syscall number (__NR_clock_gettime = 113, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:318, kernel/time/posix-timers.c:1150).\n\ndef encodeTimespecL29–32 ↗#16-byte little-endian Linux AArch64 struct timespec (tv_sec at +0, tv_nsec at +8, matching put_timespec64 in kernel/time/time.c:89).\n\ndef execClockGettimeL34–59 ↗#clock_gettime(clk_id, tp) (__NR_clock_gettime = 113, kernel/time/posix-timers.c:1150): models SYSCALL_DEFINE2(clock_gettime, ...) and the AArch64 vDSO __kernel_clock_gettime (lib/vdso/gettimeofday.c) for static POSIX clocks 0..7 (CLOCK_REALTIME = 0, CLOCK_MONOTONIC = 1, CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID = 2, CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID = 3, CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW = 4, CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE = 5, CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE = 6, CLOCK_BOOTTIME = 7):* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: if clk.toNat > 7, fails closed with .Exception \"unmodeled clock_gettime clockid\" without consuming an oracle entry;* if !s.mem.canWrite tp 16, returns EFAULT_RET (-EFAULT, matching put_timespec64 copy_to_user in kernel/time/time.c) without consuming an oracle entry;* [INTENTIONAL_OVERAPPROX]: queries ts := s.oracle.clockGettime s.oracleIdx, writes the 16-byte little-endian struct timespec (encodeTimespec ts), consumes one oracle entry via s.nextOracle, and returns 0. No monotonicity is assumed across successive calls.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Kernel/Time.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Kernel.Time/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Kernel___Time"}});